  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Egypt">
  <data key="d0">Egypt</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Egypt is the country to which Abram travels because of a severe famine in Canaan. It is the homeland of Hagar, who is repeatedly identified as Egyptian, and the land from which Hagar’s son receives a wife. Egypt also marks the geographic boundary of Ishmael’s dwelling range and is the country that the LORD instructs Isaac not to enter.

Egypt is ruled by Pharaoh and is associated with his household, royal officers, magicians, and wise men, whom Pharaoh summons to interpret his dreams. The Ishmeelites take Joseph to Egypt, where he is sold to Potiphar and where Potiphar’s household is located. Joseph later interprets Pharaoh’s dreams and governs Egypt during the years of plenty and famine. Under Pharaoh’s authority, Joseph stores and sells food, administers famine relief, acquires land for Pharaoh, and supplies the population during the crisis.

During the famine, Joseph’s brothers travel from Canaan to Egypt to buy corn and appear before him. Joseph invites Jacob and his household to settle there, providing supplies and wagons. Egypt becomes the place where Jacob’s family lives for a time, where Joseph resides, and where Ephraim and Manasseh are born before Jacob’s arrival. Joseph’s family remains there during part of the journey to bury Jacob, and Egypt is ultimately where Joseph dies and is placed in a coffin.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-054</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pharaoh">
  <data key="d0">Pharaoh</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Pharaoh is the title used for the ruler of Egypt in several narratives, likely referring to different Egyptian kings rather than one identifiable individual. In the account of Abram and Sarai, Pharaoh takes Sarai into his household, enriches Abram because of her, and later confronts and sends Abram away after divine intervention.

Another Pharaoh is the ruler served by Potiphar, who is identified as Pharaoh’s officer and captain of the guard. A further Pharaoh becomes angry with two of his officers and orders them confined in prison. The Pharaoh associated with Joseph is the Egyptian king who dreams of cattle and ears of corn, seeks an interpreter, and summons Joseph from the dungeon. After Joseph interprets the dreams, Pharaoh appoints him over Egypt and directs the people to obtain food from him.

This Pharaoh is also invoked by Joseph’s brothers when they swear that they are not spies, and Judah compares Joseph’s authority with that of Pharaoh. He welcomes Joseph’s brothers, commands them to bring their father and households to Egypt, and provides wagons from Egypt to transport Jacob and his family. He questions Jacob’s family, authorizes their settlement in Goshen, and instructs Joseph to place them in Egypt.

During the famine, Pharaoh is the ruler for whom Joseph acquires the land of Egypt, with the population thereafter owing Pharaoh a fifth of their agricultural produce. Near the end of Jacob’s life, Joseph approaches Pharaoh’s household concerning arrangements for Jacob’s burial, and Pharaoh grants Joseph permission to leave Egypt and bury his father.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-054</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
