  <data key="d2">A container Isaac instructed Esau to take while hunting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Venison">
  <data key="d0">Venison</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Venison is hunted meat prepared and presented to Isaac by Jacob and Esau as part of their requests for a blessing.&lt;SEP&gt;Hunted game Isaac requested Esau to bring for savory meat.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kids Of The Goats">
  <data key="d0">Kids Of The Goats</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Two young goats Rebekah instructed Jacob to fetch so she could prepare savory meat.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water">
  <data key="d0">Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A natural resource found by Isaac's servants in a well.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Savory Meat">
  <data key="d0">Savory Meat</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Savory meat is food prepared by Rebekah and Esau for Isaac, who eats it before giving a blessing.&lt;SEP&gt;Prepared food that Isaac requested Esau to bring and that Rebekah prepared for Isaac.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oath">
  <data key="d0">Oath</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A solemn pledge proposed by Abimelech and made between Abimelech's group and Isaac.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blessing">
  <data key="d0">Blessing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The blessing is a paternal grant of status, prosperity, authority, and protection that Isaac gives to Jacob and separately addresses to Esau.&lt;SEP&gt;A favorable declaration Isaac intended to give his son before his death.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Curse">
  <data key="d0">Curse</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A harmful consequence Jacob feared might result from deceiving Isaac.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Feast">
  <data key="d0">Feast</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A meal Isaac prepared for Abimelech and his companions before they swore to one another.&lt;SEP&gt;The feast was organized by Laban after gathering the men of the place for the marriage proceedings.&lt;SEP&gt;A celebration held by Pharaoh for all his servants on Pharaoh's birthday.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-042</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Goat Skins">
  <data key="d0">Goat Skins</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Goat skins are placed by Rebekah on Jacob's hands and neck to imitate Esau's hairy appearance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Goats">
  <data key="d0">Goats</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Goats are the animals whose kids' skins Rebekah places on Jacob's hands and neck to imitate Esau.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Corn">
  <data key="d0">Corn</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Corn is agricultural produce included among the provisions invoked in Isaac's blessing.&lt;SEP&gt;The grain gathered, stored, and sold by Joseph during the years of abundance and famine.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn is the food that Joseph's brothers receive, transport, consume, and seek to obtain during the famine.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn was purchased with money from Egypt and Canaan during the famine.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-051</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="People">
  <data key="d0">People</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">People are those whom Isaac's blessing declares will serve Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nations">
  <data key="d0">Nations</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Nations are those whom Isaac's blessing declares will bow down to Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sword">
  <data key="d0">Sword</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A sword is the means by which Isaac declares that Esau will live.&lt;SEP&gt;A sword is the weapon used by Simeon and Levi to attack the city and kill its males.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yoke">
  <data key="d0">Yoke</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A yoke symbolizes Jacob's dominion over Esau and is described as being broken from Esau's neck when Esau gains dominion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mahalath">
  <data key="d0">Mahalath</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mahalath is Ishmael's daughter, Nebajoth's sister, and Esau's wife.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="God Almighty">
  <data key="d0">God Almighty</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">God Almighty is the divine figure invoked by Isaac when blessing Jacob with fruitfulness, multiplication, and Abraham's blessing.&lt;SEP&gt;God Almighty is invoked by Israel as the giver of mercy before Joseph.&lt;SEP&gt;God Almighty appeared to Jacob at Luz in the land of Canaan, blessed Jacob, and promised Jacob descendants and an everlasting possession of the land.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-051</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Angels of God">
  <data key="d0">Angels of God</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">The angels of God are supernatural beings seen by Jacob ascending and descending on a ladder.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Haran">
  <data key="d0">Haran</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Haran is the destination associated with Laban and Jacob's refuge from Esau.&lt;SEP&gt;Haran is the place identified by the shepherds as their origin.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Luz">
  <data key="d0">Luz</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Luz is the former name of the city later called Bethel.&lt;SEP&gt;Luz is the former name of the city that Jacob called Bethel.&lt;SEP&gt;A city in Canaan identified with Bethel.&lt;SEP&gt;Luz is a place in the land of Canaan where God Almighty appeared to Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-051</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ladder">
  <data key="d0">Ladder</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The ladder is the dream structure set on earth with its top reaching heaven and with angels ascending and descending on it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stone Pillar">
  <data key="d0">Stone Pillar</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The stone pillar is the stone Jacob places upright and anoints with oil at the place later called Bethel.&lt;SEP&gt;Jacob set up a stone as a pillar between Laban and Jacob, and the pillar served as a witness and boundary.&lt;SEP&gt;A stone monument Jacob set up at the place where God spoke with Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Daughters of Heth">
  <data key="d0">Daughters of Heth</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The daughters of Heth are women whose marriages cause Rebekah distress and whose family group is contrasted with Jacob's intended marriage connection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Daughters of Canaan">
  <data key="d0">Daughters of Canaan</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The daughters of Canaan are women whom Isaac forbids Jacob to marry and whose marriages are viewed unfavorably within Isaac's family.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stone">
  <data key="d0">Stone</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The stone served Jacob as a pillow, was set up as a pillar, and was later used to cover the mouth of a well.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pillar">
  <data key="d0">Pillar</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The pillar was erected by Jacob from the stone and anointed with oil as a sacred marker.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Land Of The East">
  <data key="d0">Land Of The East</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The land of the east is the region toward which Jacob traveled.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121384</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sarah">
  <data key="d0">Sarah</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Sarah, formerly called Sarai, is Abraham’s wife and the mother of Isaac. She is described as Abraham’s paternal half-sister and as a woman who bore Isaac in old age after receiving the promise of his birth while inside her tent. She was also portrayed as a “mother of nations.”

As Isaac’s mother, Sarah objected to Hagar’s son being recognized as heir alongside Isaac. Sarah lived for 127 years and died in Kirjatharba, identified with Hebron in Canaan. Abraham buried her body in the cave of Machpelah, where she is described as being buried with him.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121386</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isaac">
  <data key="d0">Isaac</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Isaac is the son promised by God to Abraham and Sarah and born to them in Abraham’s old age. God established an everlasting covenant with Isaac, through whom Abraham’s seed would be called, and included him among the patriarchs who received the divine oath concerning the future land of the children of Israel. He was Abraham’s beloved son by Sarah and accompanied Abraham to the offering place, where he was bound on the altar before God halted the sacrifice.

Abraham instructed his servant to seek Isaac a wife from Abraham’s country and kindred, and Isaac subsequently married Rebekah, whom the servant encountered in the field. Isaac and Rebekah were the parents of Esau and Jacob. Isaac lived in Gerar, prospered there, dug wells, traveled to Beersheba, received a divine appearance, and made a covenant with Abimelech. He later ate venison, tested Jacob’s identity, blessed both Jacob and Esau, and directed Jacob not to marry a daughter of Canaan.

Isaac was Jacob’s father and was identified by Jacob as the father whose God spoke to him; Jacob also swore by Isaac’s fear and regarded Isaac among the fathers who walked before God. Jacob intended to visit him in Canaan. Isaac lived 180 years and was buried by Esau and Jacob in the Cave of Machpelah with Rebekah.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-054</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121388</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zilpah">
  <data key="d0">Zilpah</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zilpah is the maid whom Laban gave to Leah as a handmaid.&lt;SEP&gt;Zilpah was Leah's handmaid who became Jacob's wife and bore Gad and Asher.&lt;SEP&gt;Leah's handmaid and the mother of Gad and Asher.&lt;SEP&gt;Zilpah was one of Israel's wives whose sons tended the flock with Joseph.&lt;SEP&gt;Zilpah is given by Laban to Leah and bears children to Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;Zilpah was given by Laban to Leah and bore children to Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121388</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gate Of Heaven">
  <data key="d0">Gate Of Heaven</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The gate of heaven is the heavenly gateway associated with the sacred place in Jacob's declaration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121388</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Father's House">
  <data key="d0">Father's House</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob's father's house is the household to which Jacob vowed to return in peace.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121388</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oil">
  <data key="d0">Oil</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Oil was poured by Jacob onto the top of the stone pillar.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121388</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bilhah">
  <data key="d0">Bilhah</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Bilhah was Rachel's handmaid who became Jacob's wife and bore Dan and Naphtali.&lt;SEP&gt;Rachel's handmaid and Jacob's concubine, with whom Reuben lay.&lt;SEP&gt;Bilhah was one of Israel's wives whose sons tended the flock with Joseph.&lt;SEP&gt;Bilhah was given by Laban to Rachel and bore children to Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121388</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reuben">
  <data key="d0">Reuben</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Reuben was Leah's first son with Jacob and found mandrakes during the wheat harvest.&lt;SEP&gt;Jacob's firstborn son who lay with Bilhah, his father's concubine.&lt;SEP&gt;Reuben opposed killing Joseph, proposed placing Joseph in a wilderness pit, and later discovered that Joseph was missing.&lt;SEP&gt;Reuben warns against harming the child and offers his two sons as a pledge for Benjamin's safe return.&lt;SEP&gt;Reuben is Jacob's firstborn son and the father of Hanoch, Phallu, Hezron, and Carmi.&lt;SEP&gt;Reuben is Jacob's firstborn son, described as possessing might and strength but as unstable and disqualified from preeminence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121388</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simeon">
  <data key="d0">Simeon</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Simeon was Leah's second son with Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;Simeon is one of Jacob's sons and Dinah's brother who attacks Shechem with Levi and kills the city's males.&lt;SEP&gt;A son of Jacob and Leah.&lt;SEP&gt;Simeon is taken and bound by Joseph while Joseph's other brothers return for food.&lt;SEP&gt;Simeon is Joseph's brother whom Joseph's household steward brings out to the other men.&lt;SEP&gt;Simeon is a son of Jacob and the father of Jemuel, Jamin, Ohad, Jachin, Zohar, and Shaul.&lt;SEP&gt;Simeon is Jacob's son and Levi's brother, associated with fierce anger and violent actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121388</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Levi">
  <data key="d0">Levi</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Levi was Leah's third son with Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;Levi is one of Jacob's sons and Dinah's brother who attacks Shechem with Simeon and kills the city's males.&lt;SEP&gt;A son of Jacob and Leah.&lt;SEP&gt;Levi is a son of Jacob and the father of Gershon, Kohath, and Merari.&lt;SEP&gt;Levi is Jacob's son and Simeon's brother, associated with fierce anger and violent actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121388</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abraham">
  <data key="d0">Abraham</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Abraham, formerly called Abram, is a biblical patriarch, prophet, and recipient of God’s covenant and oath. God promised him numerous descendants and the land of Canaan, and Abraham is portrayed as walking before God, obeying the LORD, circumcising the males of his household, and receiving visitors at Mamre. He is the husband of Sarah, the father of Isaac and Ishmael, and later the husband of Keturah. He is also identified as an ancestor of Jacob, whose God invokes Abraham as one of Jacob’s forefathers and as the recipient of the land promised to Jacob and his descendants.

Abraham repeatedly intercedes with the LORD for the righteous in Sodom, then witnesses the destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah while God remembers him during Lot’s rescue. He resides for a time in Gerar, makes a covenant with Abimelech, and sends away Hagar and Ishmael. In a supreme test of obedience, he travels with Isaac and two young men, builds an altar, binds Isaac, and prepares a burnt offering, but offers a ram in Isaac’s place. His servants dig wells, and the LORD promises to bless and multiply Isaac’s descendants for Abraham’s sake.

Abraham purchases the field and Cave of Machpelah from Ephron the Hittite, buries Sarah there, and is later buried in the same cave. He gives his inheritance to Isaac and commissions his eldest servant to find Isaac a wife from Abraham’s kindred rather than from among the Canaanites. Thus, Abraham is presented as the foundational patriarch through whom the covenant, ancestral lineage, and promises of land and descendants continue to Isaac and Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-054</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rebekah">
  <data key="d0">Rebekah</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Rebekah was the daughter of Bethuel and the sister of Laban. She is introduced as a woman who drew water for Abraham’s servant and his camels, generously providing for them. Recognized as the woman appointed to become Isaac’s wife, she agreed to leave with the servant and married Isaac.

Rebekah was the mother of Esau and Jacob, and she favored and loved Jacob. After overhearing Isaac instruct Esau to obtain food so that Isaac could bless him, Rebekah directed Jacob to secure the food instead. She prepared savory meat and bread, dressed Jacob in Esau’s clothing, and covered his hands and neck with goat skins so that Isaac would mistake him for Esau and give him the blessing intended for the elder son.

Rebekah later warned Jacob about Esau’s plan against him and sent him to Laban for safety. She was served by Deborah as her nurse and was buried with Isaac in the Cave of Machpelah.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Naphtali">
  <data key="d0">Naphtali</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Naphtali was Bilhah's second son with Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;A son of Jacob and Bilhah.&lt;SEP&gt;Naphtali was Jacob's son and the father of Jahzeel, Guni, Jezer, and Shillem.&lt;SEP&gt;Naphtali is Jacob’s son portrayed as a hind let loose who gives goodly words.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gad">
  <data key="d0">Gad</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Gad was Zilpah's first son with Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;A son of Jacob and Zilpah.&lt;SEP&gt;Gad is a son of Jacob and the father of Ziphion, Haggi, Shuni, Ezbon, Eri, Arodi, and Areli.&lt;SEP&gt;Gad is Jacob’s son who is overcome by a troop but overcomes at the last.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Asher">
  <data key="d0">Asher</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Asher was Zilpah's second son with Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;A son of Jacob and Zilpah.&lt;SEP&gt;Asher is a son of Jacob and the father of Jimnah, Ishuah, Isui, Beriah, and Serah.&lt;SEP&gt;Asher is Jacob’s son associated with abundant bread and royal dainties.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Issachar">
  <data key="d0">Issachar</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Issachar was Leah's fifth son with Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;A son of Jacob and Leah.&lt;SEP&gt;Issachar is a son of Jacob and the father of Tola, Phuvah, Job, and Shimron.&lt;SEP&gt;Issachar is Jacob’s son portrayed as a strong ass who accepts burdens, rest, pleasant land, and tribute-bearing service.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zebulun">
  <data key="d0">Zebulun</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zebulun was Leah's sixth son with Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;A son of Jacob and Leah.&lt;SEP&gt;Zebulun is a son of Jacob and the father of Sered, Elon, and Jahleel.&lt;SEP&gt;Zebulun is Jacob’s son associated with dwelling at the haven of the sea and serving as a haven for ships.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dinah">
  <data key="d0">Dinah</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Dinah was Leah's daughter with Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;Dinah is a daughter born to an unnamed woman and given the name Dinah.&lt;SEP&gt;Dinah is Leah's daughter and Jacob's daughter who goes out to see the daughters of the land and is defiled by Shechem.&lt;SEP&gt;Dinah is Jacob's daughter and the sister of Simeon and Levi; Dinah is taken from Shechem's house after Shechem defiles her.&lt;SEP&gt;Dinah is Jacob's daughter and a daughter of Leah.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mandrakes">
  <data key="d0">Mandrakes</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Mandrakes were plants found by Reuben during the wheat harvest and used in an arrangement between Leah and Rachel concerning Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Laban's Sons">
  <data key="d0">Laban's Sons</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Laban’s sons manage livestock assigned by Laban and accuse Jacob of taking their father’s property.&lt;SEP&gt;Laban's sons were kissed by Laban when Laban departed and returned to his place.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Laban's Flocks">
  <data key="d0">Laban's Flocks</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Laban’s flocks consist of cattle, sheep, and goats managed by Jacob and separated according to animal markings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jacob's Flocks">
  <data key="d0">Jacob's Flocks</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob’s flocks are the stronger and increasingly numerous livestock distinguished from Laban’s cattle.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wages">
  <data key="d0">Wages</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Wages are the compensation negotiated between Jacob and Laban for Jacob’s continued service and livestock management.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rods">
  <data key="d0">Rods</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Rods made from poplar, hazel, and chestnut wood are peeled and placed near watering troughs during livestock breeding.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Land of Jacob's Fathers">
  <data key="d0">Land of Jacob's Fathers</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The ancestral land to which God commands Jacob to return.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Angel Of God">
  <data key="d0">Angel Of God</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">The angel of God spoke to Jacob in a dream about the patterned rams and God's awareness of Laban's actions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mount Gilead">
  <data key="d0">Mount Gilead</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Mount Gilead was the region Jacob reached while fleeing and where Laban overtook and camped against him.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rams">
  <data key="d0">Rams</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">The rams that leaped upon the cattle appeared in Jacob's dream as ringstraked, speckled, and grisled.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Images">
  <data key="d0">Images</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The images were Laban's household objects that Rachel stole and concealed in camel furniture.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Camel Furniture">
  <data key="d0">Camel Furniture</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Camel furniture held the household images and supported Rachel while Laban searched.&lt;SEP&gt;Camel furniture was the carrying equipment in which Rachel concealed Laban's images.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jacob's Sons">
  <data key="d0">Jacob's Sons</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob's sons traveled with Jacob and were carried on camels during Jacob's departure from Laban.&lt;SEP&gt;Jacob's sons work with the cattle in the field and become grieved and angry after hearing about Dinah.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jacob's Wives">
  <data key="d0">Jacob's Wives</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob's wives traveled with Jacob and were carried on camels during Jacob's departure from Laban.&lt;SEP&gt;Jacob's wives are the two wives whom Jacob takes across the ford of Jabbok.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Angels Of God">
  <data key="d0">Angels Of God</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">The angels of God met Jacob on Jacob's journey.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Household Images">
  <data key="d0">Household Images</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The household images were taken by Rachel and hidden in camel furniture while Laban searched for them.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leah's Tent">
  <data key="d0">Leah's Tent</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Leah's tent was searched by Laban during the search for the household images.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rachel's Tent">
  <data key="d0">Rachel's Tent</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Rachel's tent was entered and searched by Laban during the search for the household images.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heap Of Stones">
  <data key="d0">Heap Of Stones</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob's brethren gathered stones into a heap that served as a witness and boundary between Laban and Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jegarsahadutha">
  <data key="d0">Jegarsahadutha</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Jegarsahadutha was the name Laban gave to the stone heap.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Galeed">
  <data key="d0">Galeed</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Galeed was the name Jacob gave to the stone heap and the name associated with the covenant witness.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mizpah">
  <data key="d0">Mizpah</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Mizpah was a name associated with the witness arrangement between Laban and Jacob, invoking divine oversight during separation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mahanaim">
  <data key="d0">Mahanaim</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Mahanaim was the place Jacob named after seeing God's host.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Seir">
  <data key="d0">Seir</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Seir was the land where Jacob's messengers were sent to find Esau.&lt;SEP&gt;Seir is the destination toward which Esau returns and where Jacob says Jacob will later meet Esau.&lt;SEP&gt;Seir is identified as the ancestor of the Horites who inhabited the land.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mount">
  <data key="d0">Mount</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The mount was the place where Jacob offered sacrifice and where Jacob and his brethren ate and stayed overnight.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sacrifice">
  <data key="d0">Sacrifice</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob offered a sacrifice upon the mount before calling his brethren to eat.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jacob's Brethren">
  <data key="d0">Jacob's Brethren</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob's brethren gathered stones, ate at the stone heap, and shared bread with Jacob on the mount.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Messengers">
  <data key="d0">Messengers</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Messengers are Jacob's representatives who return from meeting Esau and report that Esau is approaching with four hundred men.&lt;SEP&gt;The messengers were sent by Jacob to Esau and later returned to Jacob with news of Esau's approach.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jacob's Stuff">
  <data key="d0">Jacob's Stuff</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob's stuff was searched by Laban during the dispute over the household images.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Four Hundred Men">
  <data key="d0">Four Hundred Men</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Four hundred men accompanied Esau as Esau came to meet Jacob.&lt;SEP&gt;Four hundred men accompany Esau when Esau approaches Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Womenservants">
  <data key="d0">Womenservants</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Womenservants are female servants belonging to Jacob and included among Jacob's household.&lt;SEP&gt;Jacob reported possessing womenservants when sending word to Esau.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121389</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Laban">
  <data key="d0">Laban</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Laban was a Syrian (Aramean), the son of Bethuel and Nahor, and the brother of Rebekah. Associated with Haran, he participated with Bethuel in approving Rebekah’s departure and marriage. He ran to meet Abraham’s servant, provided lodging and provisions, and helped arrange Rebekah’s union with Isaac.

Laban was the father of Leah and Rachel and received Jacob when Jacob came to stay with him. He became Jacob’s employer and father-in-law, providing his daughters’ handmaids, Bilhah and Zilpah—Bilhah to Rachel and Zilpah to Leah. Laban controlled substantial flocks, negotiated and repeatedly changed Jacob’s wages, and separated livestock belonging to Jacob from his own.

After Jacob departed, Laban pursued him, searched the tents for stolen household images, accused Jacob, and asserted ownership over his daughters, children, and cattle. Despite their dispute, Laban and Jacob made a covenant, after which Laban departed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="God (LORD)">
  <data key="d0">God (LORD)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">God, also called the LORD, is invoked by Jacob, directs Jacob to return to his country, promises to deal well with Jacob, and promises numerous descendants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Wrestling Man">
  <data key="d0">The Wrestling Man</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Wrestling Man struggles with Jacob until daybreak, injures Jacob's thigh, blesses Jacob, and gives Jacob the name Israel.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jabbok">
  <data key="d0">Jabbok</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Jabbok is the ford or waterway that Jacob crosses with Jacob's household and possessions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peniel (Penuel)">
  <data key="d0">Peniel (Penuel)</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Peniel, also called Penuel, is the place Jacob names after seeing God face to face and surviving.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Livestock Present for Esau">
  <data key="d0">Livestock Present for Esau</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The livestock present consists of goats, sheep, camels, cattle, asses, and their young, arranged in separate droves for Esau.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Children of Israel">
  <data key="d0">Children of Israel</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Children of Israel are the group described as observing a prohibition against eating the sinew that shrank on Jacob's thigh.&lt;SEP&gt;The children of Israel are described as avoiding the sinew that shrank on the hollow of the thigh.&lt;SEP&gt;The Children of Israel are the people over whom no king had yet reigned when the listed kings ruled in Edom.&lt;SEP&gt;The children of Israel received Joseph's oath concerning the future transportation of Joseph's bones.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-054</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sinew Dietary Prohibition">
  <data key="d0">Sinew Dietary Prohibition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Sinew Dietary Prohibition is the practice of not eating the sinew located on the hollow of the thigh.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jacob's Eleven Sons">
  <data key="d0">Jacob's Eleven Sons</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob's eleven sons are the children whom Jacob takes across the ford of Jabbok.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Esau's Four Hundred Men">
  <data key="d0">Esau's Four Hundred Men</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Esau's four hundred men accompany Esau when Esau approaches Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Servants">
  <data key="d0">Servants</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Servants receive and lead Jacob's separate droves of livestock ahead of Jacob toward Esau.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shechem (Person)">
  <data key="d0">Shechem (Person)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Shechem is Hamor's son, a Hivite prince who takes Dinah, lies with her, loves her, and asks Hamor to arrange her marriage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hamor">
  <data key="d0">Hamor</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Hamor is Shechem's father who communes with Jacob and Jacob's sons about Shechem's relationship with Dinah and proposed marriages.&lt;SEP&gt;Hamor is Shechem's father who seeks marriage between Shechem and Dinah and negotiates with Jacob's sons and the men of his city.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Handmaidens">
  <data key="d0">Handmaidens</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The handmaidens are women accompanying Jacob's household who approach Esau with their children and bow.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hivites">
  <data key="d0">Hivites</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hivites are the people associated with Shechem's identity and status as a prince.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Succoth">
  <data key="d0">Succoth</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Succoth is the place where Jacob journeys, builds a house, and makes booths for cattle.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shalem">
  <data key="d0">Shalem</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Shalem is the city where Jacob arrives after leaving Padanaram and pitches his tent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shechem (City)">
  <data key="d0">Shechem (City)</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Shechem is the city identified with Shalem and located in the land of Canaan.&lt;SEP&gt;Shechem is the city whose inhabitants accept circumcision and whose males are killed and whose wealth is taken by the sons of Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Children of Hamor">
  <data key="d0">Children of Hamor</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The children of Hamor hold the field that Jacob purchases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EleloheIsrael">
  <data key="d0">EleloheIsrael</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">EleloheIsrael is the altar erected and named by Jacob.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sinew That Shrank">
  <data key="d0">Sinew That Shrank</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">The sinew that shrank is located upon the hollow of the thigh and is avoided by the children of Israel.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hollow Of The Thigh">
  <data key="d0">Hollow Of The Thigh</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">The hollow of the thigh is the body location associated with the sinew that shrank.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Daughters Of The Land">
  <data key="d0">Daughters Of The Land</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The daughters of the land are the women whom Dinah goes out to see.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Present">
  <data key="d0">Present</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The present is a gift that Jacob asks Esau to receive as a sign of favor and reconciliation.&lt;SEP&gt;The present consists of selected land produce and aromatics, including balm, honey, spices, myrrh, nuts, and almonds, and is brought to Joseph.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-046</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flocks And Herds">
  <data key="d0">Flocks And Herds</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">The flocks and herds accompanying Jacob include animals with young and require gentle travel.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="House">
  <data key="d0">House</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The house is a dwelling that Jacob builds at Succoth.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Booths">
  <data key="d0">Booths</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The booths are structures that Jacob makes for his cattle at Succoth.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One Hundred Pieces Of Money">
  <data key="d0">One Hundred Pieces Of Money</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">One hundred pieces of money are the stated payment Jacob gives for the parcel of field.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shechem">
  <data key="d0">Shechem</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Shechem is Hamor's son who desires Dinah, requests Dinah as his wife, accepts circumcision, and is killed by Simeon and Levi.&lt;SEP&gt;A place near an oak where Jacob hid strange gods and earrings.&lt;SEP&gt;Shechem was the place where Joseph's brothers tended their father's flock and where Joseph went to find them.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marriage Alliance">
  <data key="d0">Marriage Alliance</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Marriage alliances are proposed between Hamor's people and Jacob's family through exchanges of daughters as wives.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strange Gods">
  <data key="d0">Strange Gods</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Strange gods are objects kept by members of Jacob's household and surrendered to Jacob for concealment.&lt;SEP&gt;Idols held by Jacob's household that were surrendered and hidden by Jacob under an oak near Shechem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Earrings">
  <data key="d0">Earrings</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Earrings are personal objects surrendered by Jacob's household and hidden under an oak near Shechem.&lt;SEP&gt;Personal ornaments taken from Jacob's household and hidden by Jacob under an oak near Shechem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jacob's Household">
  <data key="d0">Jacob's Household</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob's household consists of the people accompanying Jacob, who are instructed to remove strange gods, cleanse themselves, change garments, and journey to Bethel.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sons of Jacob">
  <data key="d0">Sons of Jacob</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The sons of Jacob negotiate with Hamor and Shechem, impose circumcision as a condition for alliance, attack the city, and take its possessions and inhabitants captive.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oak">
  <data key="d0">Oak</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The oak is the tree under which Jacob hides the strange gods and earrings surrendered by his household near Shechem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elbethel">
  <data key="d0">Elbethel</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The name Jacob gave to the place where God appeared to Jacob when Jacob fled from his brother.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deborah">
  <data key="d0">Deborah</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Rebekah's nurse who died and was buried beneath an oak near Bethel.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Allonbachuth">
  <data key="d0">Allonbachuth</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The name given to the place where Deborah was buried.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bethlehem">
  <data key="d0">Bethlehem</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The place identified as Ephrath where Rachel was buried.&lt;SEP&gt;Bethlehem is identified in the passage as the same place as Ephrath.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121390</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Esau">
  <data key="d0">Esau</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Esau was Isaac and Rebekah’s elder twin and firstborn son, and Jacob’s brother. Described as a cunning or skilled hunter and a man of the field, he prepared savory venison for Isaac. Esau sold his birthright to Jacob in exchange for bread and lentil pottage, and he later reacted bitterly when Jacob received Isaac’s blessing, which Esau had been intended to inherit.

Afterward, Esau hated Jacob and threatened to kill him. When Jacob returned, Esau traveled to meet him with four hundred men. The encounter ended in reconciliation: Esau embraced Jacob, accepted his present, and then returned toward Seir. Esau’s presence is also recalled when God directed Jacob to build an altar at Bethel.

Esau was associated with Edom and is regarded as the father of the Edomites. He eventually moved away from Jacob with his household and was connected with Mount Seir, where he resided. The traditions name his wives variously as Judith, Bashemath, Mahalath, Adah, and Aholibamah; Mahalath is identified as Ishmael’s daughter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121392</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Benoni">
  <data key="d0">Benoni</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The name Rachel gave to her son during her dying moments before Jacob called the son Benjamin.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121392</data>
