<node id="Rachel">
  <data key="d0">Rachel</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Rachel was Laban’s younger daughter, a shepherdess, and Leah’s sister. She became Jacob’s beloved wife after he sought to marry her, and she is associated with Jacob’s household and family. Rachel was also the mistress of Bilhah, whom she gave to Jacob as a secondary wife or concubine.

Initially barren, Rachel conceived after God remembered her and opened her womb, giving birth to Joseph. She was later the mother of Benjamin as well. During Jacob’s approach to Esau, Rachel was among the women to whom Jacob’s children were assigned, and she appears with Joseph during the family’s meeting with Esau.

When Jacob departed from Laban, Rachel stole her father’s household images and concealed them in camel furniture. She remained seated while Laban searched the tent, preventing him from discovering them. Rachel eventually died in Canaan near Ephrath while giving birth to Benjamin and was buried there, near Bethlehem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121394</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jacob">
  <data key="d0">Jacob</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Jacob, also called Israel, is the younger twin son of Isaac and Rebekah, Esau’s brother, and a plain-dwelling patriarch associated with tents. He acquired Esau’s birthright and, with Rebekah’s preparation—including goat kids—disguised himself as Esau to receive Isaac’s blessing. Fleeing Esau, Jacob traveled to Padanaram, where he dreamed of a ladder reaching heaven and encountered Rachel.

Jacob served Laban for twenty years, working for his daughters and managing Laban’s cattle and flocks. He married Leah, Rachel, Bilhah, and Zilpah, fathering twelve sons and other children, including Dinah. Through negotiated wages and careful management, he gained his own livestock. After God commanded him to return to his ancestral land, Jacob fled with his family and possessions, disputed Laban’s accusations, and made a covenant with him.

Fearing Esau, Jacob prayed, sent him livestock as a gift, crossed the Jordan and Jabbok, and wrestled with an unnamed man, receiving the name Israel. He then met Esau and journeyed with his household through Succoth and Shalem, where he bought land and built an altar. After Dinah’s ordeal involving Hamor and Shechem and the actions of her brothers, God directed Jacob to Bethel, where he erected altars and pillars.

Jacob lived in Canaan, separated from Esau when their possessions became too extensive for them to dwell together, and buried Rachel and Isaac. He was Joseph’s father and mourned him after recognizing his bloodied coat and believing he had been devoured. During famine, Jacob sent his sons to Egypt for food and eventually moved there with his household, including Benjamin. In Egypt he became the grandfather of Ephraim and Manasseh through Joseph, blessed his sons and descendants, assigned inheritances, and requested burial in the cave of Machpelah. He died after gathering his feet into his bed. Jacob is also identified as a recipient of God’s oath concerning the future land of Israel’s descendants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-054</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121395</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leah">
  <data key="d0">Leah</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Leah was the elder daughter of Laban, Rachel’s sister, and one of Jacob’s wives. Laban gave Leah to Jacob in place of Rachel, whom Jacob intended to marry, making her part of the complicated family history of Jacob’s marriages. Leah later joined Rachel in meeting Jacob in the field and questioned the inheritance available to them from their father’s household.

Leah was the mother of Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Judah, Issachar, Zebulun, and Dinah. Her children are associated with Jacob’s descendants who later went to Egypt. During Jacob’s approach to Esau, Leah and her children were assigned a designated place within the household arrangement, and Leah was identified among the women and children under Jacob’s protection.

Leah received Zilpah from Laban, and Zilpah is associated with Leah’s household. Leah is also connected with the tent searched by Laban during his pursuit of Jacob’s family. After her death, Leah was buried in the Cave of Machpelah.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121395</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Judah">
  <data key="d0">Judah</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Judah was the fourth son of Jacob and Leah, and the brother of Joseph and Benjamin. He was the father of Er, Onan, Shelah, Pharez, and Zarah. Judah proposed selling Joseph to the Ishmeelites rather than killing him, after which he departed from his brothers, went to Hirah, and married the daughter of Shuah.

Judah was Tamar’s father-in-law through his son Er and the father of Shelah. After failing to give Tamar Shelah as promised, Judah encountered her on the way to Timnath without recognizing her and fathered her twins, Pharez and Zarah. When Tamar’s pregnancy was revealed, Judah acknowledged that she was more righteous than he was.

During the family’s efforts to obtain food from Egypt, Judah reported Joseph’s condition that Benjamin had to accompany the brothers before they could return. He urged Jacob to send Benjamin and pledged responsibility for his safe return. When the brothers later stood before Joseph, Judah spoke on Benjamin’s behalf, pleaded for mercy, and offered himself and his brothers as servants.

Jacob sent Judah ahead of the family to direct Joseph toward Goshen. In Jacob’s blessing, Judah is portrayed as one whom his brothers will praise, victorious over enemies, and symbolized by a lion. He is also associated with enduring rule and the gathering of peoples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121398</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Israel">
  <data key="d0">Israel</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Israel is the name God gave Jacob after Jacob prevailed in a struggle with a man and with God, replacing his former name. As the ancestral figure Israel, Jacob is the father of the twelve tribes, whose descendants are identified as the sons of Israel.

In the accounts concerning Joseph, Israel is Joseph’s father and loved Joseph more than his other children, giving him a special coat and sending him to find his brothers. He is also identified as the father of Judah, Benjamin, Joseph, and Simeon, and directs his sons to return to Egypt with Benjamin. When Joseph encounters him in Goshen, Jacob is referred to by the name Israel.

Israel appears again in the account of Jacob’s death: Joseph orders the embalming of the body of Israel, the patriarch whose sons became the founders of the twelve tribes. Israel also blesses Joseph’s sons, Ephraim and Manasseh. In addition to referring to Jacob personally, the name Israel denotes the people descended from him who lived and multiplied in Egypt’s land of Goshen.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121399</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joseph">
  <data key="d0">Joseph</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Joseph was the son of Jacob (Israel) and Rachel, whom Rachel named. He was one of Jacob’s younger sons and approached Esau with Rachel. At seventeen, Joseph tended the flock, reported his brothers’ misconduct, received preferential treatment from Jacob, and experienced dreams suggesting that he would exercise authority. His brothers called him a dreamer, cast him into a pit, and sold him to Ishmaelites, who brought him to Egypt.

In Egypt, Joseph was purchased by Potiphar and appointed overseer of his household and possessions. After refusing Potiphar’s wife’s advances, he was falsely accused and imprisoned in a dungeon, where he interpreted the dreams of the chief butler and chief baker. Later summoned by Pharaoh, Joseph interpreted Pharaoh’s dreams as a prophecy of seven plentiful years followed by seven years of famine. Pharaoh appointed him governor and ruler under Pharaoh over Egypt. Joseph stored grain during the abundant years and distributed or sold it during the famine, acquiring land, livestock, and agricultural revenue for Pharaoh while administering Egypt’s resources.

Joseph’s brothers came before him seeking food, though they did not recognize him. He tested their honesty, questioned them, bound Simeon, commanded provisions, restored their money, and arranged a meal while directing his steward and investigating the silver cup. Eventually he revealed himself, forgave them, and brought Jacob and the entire family to Egypt, providing wagons, food, and settlement in the land. He presented the family to Pharaoh, nourished Jacob’s household, and continued caring for them during the famine.

Joseph was the father of Ephraim and Manasseh, whom he presented to Jacob for blessing. Jacob described him as a fruitful bough, protected and blessed by God. Joseph arranged Jacob’s embalming and burial, returned to Egypt, reassured and supported his brothers after Jacob’s death, and died at the age of 110.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-052&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-054</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121399</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Benjamin">
  <data key="d0">Benjamin</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Benjamin is Jacob’s youngest son and Rachel’s second child. Rachel named him Benoni before her death, but Jacob renamed him Benjamin. He was Joseph’s full brother and was initially kept in Canaan by Jacob rather than sent with his brothers to Egypt, reflecting Jacob’s concern for his safety.

During the famine, Joseph required Benjamin’s presence before providing further assistance to the family. Benjamin eventually traveled with his brothers to Egypt after an older brother pledged his safety. Joseph secretly placed his silver cup in Benjamin’s sack, causing him to be accused of theft; Benjamin’s sack was also found to contain the cup after Joseph had given him a portion five times larger than those of his brothers. When Joseph revealed his identity, he gave Benjamin three hundred pieces of silver and five changes of clothing.

In genealogical tradition, Benjamin is described as the father of Belah, Becher, Ashbel, Gera, Naaman, Ehi, Rosh, Muppim, Huppim, and Ard. Jacob’s prophetic blessing portrays Benjamin as a wolf that devours prey in the morning and divides the spoil at night.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a32a41554714252668fa190ace9e2f6c-chunk-053</data>
  <data key="d4">Book 01        Genesis.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1790121399</data>
